{"qid":"Q783","names":["Honduras"],"title":"Honduras","endonym":{"native":"República de Honduras","lang":"es"},"capsule":"Honduras gained independence from Spain on September 15, 1821 as part of the Central American federation, briefly joined the Mexican Empire under Agustín I, and then participated in the United Provinces of Central America until that federation dissolved in 1838–1839, with Honduras declaring full separate sovereignty in 1840. The country's nineteenth and twentieth centuries were marked by endemic political instability — over 300 governments in roughly 150 years — punctuated by US military interventions, the 1969 Football War with El Salvador, and Cold War counterinsurgency in the 1980s when Honduras served as a base for Contra operations against Nicaragua. Since the 1990s Honduras has maintained constitutional civilian government, though a 2009 coup and persistent structural poverty and gang violence have characterized recent decades.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Honduras's territorial extent as a modern state is precisely defined by internationally recognized borders; the from date of 1840 in the dataset reflects the effective independence date after Central American federation dissolution.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Honduras","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Honduras"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Honduras","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honduras"},{"label":"Wikipedia: History of Honduras","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Honduras"}],"britannica":"place/Honduras","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Honduras","rulers":[{"qid":"Q887862","name":"Fra